Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 01:28:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/7] Add GPU governor as a kernel control type

The kernel manager exposed GPU min/max frequency but had no governor
control, because no TYPE_GPU_GOVERNOR existed and neither the <gpu> XML
path nor the sysfs autodiscovery fallback ever looked at the governor
nodes. GPU devfreq exposes the same governor/available_governors pair
cpufreq does, so the control is built the same way the CPU one is: the
value is an index into the available list, with the label strings
doubling as the values written back to sysfs.

Covers both discovery paths - governorNode/governorAvailablePath
attributes on <gpu>, and the kgsl (devfreq/governor) and generic devfreq
(governor) fallbacks.

Verified on a MediaTek MT6768 (Mali-G52) that the devfreq governor is
genuinely wired to the hardware, by cross-checking the real frequency
reported by /sys/kernel/ged/hal/current_freqency while switching
governors: powersave pinned the GPU to its 315MHz minimum and
performance raised it, while the devfreq cur_freq node is stale and
lags one read behind, which makes it look inert if trusted alone.

Change-Id: Ia33324e39c19c7df84aece7f7d3609aeaf15b7fd
